                "text": "**Analyzing the Input**\n\nI'm focusing on parsing the input format. Specifically, I'm identifying the key elements: the background color data, and the crucial \"special points\" with their row and column coordinates. My current approach involves systematically extracting and interpreting this information to build a clear understanding of the data structure. I'm noting specific examples, such as the train 1 data at (4,7) and (7,2), as this will determine the output.\n\n\n**Identifying Patterns**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on a pattern. The \"special points\" have diagonals with a value of 3. Row and column positions are 1, and the point itself is 2! I've confirmed this with the second train and I'm further validating the pattern with train 3. I'm focusing on edge case scenarios like row and col overlapping with 1. Also I now know that it is not necessarily all the diagonals around a point that are color 3, but the 2 diagonals. I am not yet sure if all points are 2.\n\n\n**Refining the Drawing Logic**\n\nI'm now iterating the drawing order. It looks like each \"special point\" (r, c) draws a vertical line (col c, color 1), a horizontal line (row r, color 1), diagonals (r\u00b11, c\u00b11, color 3), and the point itself (r, c, color 2). I'm questioning if there's any overwrite rule with the colors. Does the horizontal/vertical (color 1) overwrite the diagonals (color 3), or vice versa? I'm examining Train 3, row 0 to test these hypotheses.\n\n\n**Clarifying Line Overwrites**\n\nI've just figured out how the lines and diagonals interact. The key is that a diagonal from one point will never overlap the lines (row/col) of the same point, as they are offset. But I need to check whether a diagonal line from one point can overwrite the line of another point, or vice versa, and I'm currently checking this with the provided examples, especially Train 3. I've validated that the row/col lines are 1 and diagonals are 3, but the overwrite behavior remains uncl